ITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n2161,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"**Response Surface Design: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nResponse Surface Design (RSD) is a statistical technique employed to model and analyze the relationship between multiple explanatory variables (factors) and one or more response variables (outputs). The primary purpose of RSD is to optimize processes by exploring the effects of the factors on the response, identifying optimal conditions, and understanding the interactions between variables.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe general mathematical model for a response surface can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = f(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k) + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( f \\) is a function of the explanatory variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the random error. The function \\( f \\) is typically approximated using polynomial regression, often a second-order polynomial:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j \\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients, and \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nResponse surface designs can be structured in various ways, including Central Composite Designs (CCD), Box-Behnken Designs, and Full Factorial Designs. These designs allow for the systematic exploration of the response surface by varying the levels of the factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\n1. The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent.\n4. The experimental region is adequately explored, and the design points are well-distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResponse surface designs are particularly useful when:\n- The researcher seeks to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- There is a need to understand the interaction effects between factors.\n- The experimental region is not well-defined, and exploratory analysis is required.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a regression model to the collected data, assessing the significance of the factors and their interactions, and using diagnostic plots to validate model assumptions. Techniques such as Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) are employed to evaluate the model fit and identify significant predictors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- RSD allows for the exploration of complex relationships between multiple factors and responses.\n- It provides a systematic approach to optimization, reducing the number of experiments needed compared to full factorial designs.\n- The methodology is flexible and can be adapted to various experimental contexts.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The requirement for a well-defined experimental region can limit applicability.\n- The assumption of a polynomial relationship may not hold true in all cases, leading to model misspecification.\n- The complexity of the design and analysis may require advanced statistical knowledge.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nResponse surface designs are related to other experimental designs such as factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods. Alternatives include optimization techniques like genetic algorithms and simulated annealing, which do not rely on a predefined response surface.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing RSD, researchers should consider the selection of appropriate factor levels, the number of replications for estimating experimental error, and the need for randomization to mitigate bias. It is also essential to ensure that the experimental conditions are controlled to minimize variability.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing response surface experiments, fitting models, and conducting ANOVA. These tools facilitate the analysis and visualization of response surfaces.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nResponse surface designs are widely used in fields such as engineering, pharmaceuticals, agriculture, and food science. Common applications include optimizing chemical reactions, improving product formulations, and enhancing manufacturing processes.\",12\r\n2333,\"MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK\",\"**MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK: A Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nMyers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook are prominent authors recognized for their significant contributions to the fields of experimental design and response surface methodology (RSM). Their work primarily focuses on the development and application of statistical techniques that enhance process robustness and optimize responses in various experimental settings.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile specific mathematical formulations are not detailed in the descriptions, response surface methodology typically involves fitting a polynomial model to the response variable as a function of the input factors. The general form of a second-order polynomial model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( X_i \\) are the input factors, \\( \\beta \\) are the coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe design structures advocated by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook include various types of response surface designs, such as central composite designs (CCD) and Box-Behnken designs. These designs are structured to efficiently explore the relationships between multiple factors and their effects on the response variable.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the methodologies proposed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook relies on several assumptions, including:\n- The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial.\n- The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n- Homoscedasticity, meaning constant variance of errors across all levels of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResponse surface methodology is applicable when the goal is to optimize a response influenced by several variables. It is particularly useful in situations where:\n- The relationship between factors and responses is not well understood.\n- There is a need to identify optimal conditions for a process.\n- The number of factors is moderate, allowing for comprehensive exploration without excessive experimentation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a regression model to the collected data, followed by diagnostic checks for model adequacy. Techniques such as ANOVA are employed to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe methodologies developed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook offer several advantages:\n- They provide a systematic approach to optimizing processes.\n- The designs are efficient, often requiring fewer experimental runs than full factorial designs.\n- They facilitate the exploration of interactions between factors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nDespite their strengths, there are limitations:\n- The need for a reasonable approximation of the response surface; if the true relationship is highly nonlinear, polynomial models may not suffice.\n- The complexity of the analysis increases with the number of factors and interactions considered.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods for robust parameter design. Each of these approaches has its own strengths and is chosen based on specific experimental goals.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing the methodologies, practitioners should consider the following:\n- The selection of appropriate factor levels and ranges.\n- The need for replication to ensure reliability of results.\n- The importance of randomization to mitigate bias.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile specific software implementations are not mentioned in the descriptions, popular statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for conducting response surface methodology and analyzing experimental designs.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of the methodologies developed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook include:\n- Optimization of manufacturing processes.\n- Development of new products in various industries, including pharmaceuticals and food production.\n- Studies aimed at improving quality and performance in engineering and scientific research.\n\nIn summary, Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook have made substantial contributions to the fields of experimental design and response surface methodology, providing valuable frameworks and techniques for optimizing processes and enhancing robustness in various applications.\",5\r\n2332,KHURI AND CORNELL,\"**Summary of Khuri and Cornell in Response Surface Methodology**\n\nKhuri and Cornell are prominent authors recognized for their significant contributions to the field of Response Surface Methodology (RSM), particularly through their comprehensive presentation published in 1996. Their work serves as a foundational reference for understanding and applying response surface designs in experimental statistics.\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nResponse Surface Methodology is a collection of mathematical and statistical techniques used for modeling and analyzing problems in which a response of interest is influenced by several variables. The primary purpose of RSM is to optimize this response by exploring the relationships between the response and the independent variables, often through the use of polynomial regression models.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nThe typical model structure in RSM involves fitting a polynomial equation to the response variable \\( Y \\) as a function of the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\). A common form of the second-order polynomial model is given by:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients, \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nRSM typically employs a central composite design (CCD) or Box-Behnken design to efficiently explore the response surface. These designs allow for the estimation of the coefficients in the polynomial model while minimizing the number of experimental runs required.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of RSM relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the response and the independent variables is adequately captured by the polynomial model.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent of one another.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRSM is applicable when:\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- The relationship between the factors and the response is expected to be nonlinear.\n- Preliminary experiments have been conducted to identify significant factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of RSM typically involves:\n1. Fitting the polynomial model to the experimental data using regression analysis.\n2. Conducting hypothesis tests to evaluate the significance of the model terms.\n3. Performing diagnostic checks to validate model assumptions.\n4. Using response surface plots to visualize the relationships and identify optimal conditions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe strengths of RSM include:\n- Efficient exploration of the response surface with fewer experimental runs compared to full factorial designs.\n- Ability to identify interaction effects and nonlinear relationships.\n- Provides a systematic approach to optimization.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nLimitations of RSM include:\n- The requirement for a well-defined experimental region, as extrapolation beyond the studied range can lead to unreliable predictions.\n- The polynomial model may not adequately capture complex relationships if the true response surface is highly nonlinear or has multiple peaks.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methodologies include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and other optimization techniques such as Taguchi methods. Each of these approaches has its own strengths and is suitable for different experimental contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing RSM, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate levels for each factor to ensure a comprehensive exploration of the response surface.\n- The need for replication to assess the variability in the response.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for conducting RSM analyses, including design generation, model fitting, and visualization of response surfaces.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nRSM is widely used in various fields, including engineering, pharmaceuticals, and agriculture, for optimizing processes such as chemical reactions, product formulations, and manufacturing processes. Khuri and Cornell's work serves as a critical reference for practitioners seeking to apply RSM effectively in their experimental designs.\",4\r\n2398,VARIANCE DISPERSION GRAPH,The Variance Dispersion Graph (VDG) is a graphical representation used to display prediction variance for design and response models.,4\r\n2403,DESIGN POINT,,1\r\n2408,A-OPTIMALITY CRITERION,The A-optimality criterion minimizes the sum of the variances of the regression coefficients in an experimental design.,1\r\n2397,GIOVANNITTI-JENSEN,Giovannitti-Jensen is a researcher who developed the variance dispersion graph (VDG) in 1989.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3310,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,CCD,Response Surface Design includes Central Composite Design (CCD) as a method for modeling,21\r\n3277,\"MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK\",RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook contributed significantly to the understanding and application of response surface designs\",17\r\n3275,KHURI AND CORNELL,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"Khuri and Cornell's work is foundational in the field of response surface design, providing methodologies and insights\",16\r\n3308,VARIANCE DISPERSION GRAPH,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,The Variance Dispersion Graph (VDG) is used in the context of Response Surface Design to analyze prediction variance,16\r\n3311,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,DESIGN POINT,Design points are evaluated within the context of Response Surface Design to determine the response of the model,13\r\n3331,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,A-OPTIMALITY CRITERION,The A-optimality criterion is another method used to assess response surface designs,13\r\n3306,GIOVANNITTI-JENSEN,VARIANCE DISPERSION GRAPH,Giovannitti-Jensen developed the Variance Dispersion Graph (VDG) in 1989,5\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLAN